Thank you for the fix, the analysis in #65447 made it easy to verify.

I reproduced the race with a probe that runs 16 concurrent cold-cache warmups against a shared log: on 6.4 it aborts boots in 16 out of 20 rounds with the exact errors from the issue, with your patch it stays clean over 30 rounds. The lock also fixes a silent data loss the issue did not mention: without it, the rounds that happened to not corrupt the file still kept only 0 to 4 of the 8 distinct deprecation payloads, while the locked merge keeps all 8.

I found one gap and pushed a rework for it, squashed into your commit: a log that is already corrupted still aborted every boot, because the `unserialize()` warning throws before `ftruncate()` can repair the file. That covers files corrupted before this fix is deployed, and files left behind by a process killed between `ftruncate()` and `fwrite()`, which is not atomic; worker managers that kill workers on timeout make that a real path. The read now discards invalid content under a silencing error handler, so the file is rewritten and boots self-heal.

That scenario is deterministic, so it also gave us the test that was missing: it boots `warmUp()` in a child PHP process, since `PHPUNIT_COMPOSER_INSTALL` disables the collect path in-process. It fails on the previous head of this PR and passes now.

